# Install as /etc/sudoers.d/reticle-deploy, owned by root, mode 0440:
#
#     sudo install -o root -g root -m 0440 \
#          deploy/reticle-deploy.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/reticle-deploy
#     sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/reticle-deploy
#
# Check it with visudo before you trust it. A syntax error in a file under
# sudoers.d can make sudo refuse to run at all, and the machine you would use to
# fix that is the machine you just locked yourself out of.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are two accounts, and they are not the same account.
#
#   reticle         runs the application. Created with `--system --shell
#                   /usr/sbin/nologin`: it owns the databases' credentials and
#                   the media, and nobody logs in as it, ever.
#
#   reticle-deploy  receives the deploy over SSH. It has a real shell, because
#                   an account with no shell cannot accept an ssh command, and
#                   it owns /opt/reticle/releases so unpacking a release and
#                   building a virtualenv need no privilege at all.
#
# The two rules below are the entire gap between them. Anything a deploy does
# that is not one of these it does as itself.
#
# Be clear-eyed about the second rule: running an arbitrary command as `reticle`
# is, in practice, everything `reticle` can do — including reading every
# facility's database password. That is not a leak in the rule, it is what a
# deploy *is*: it installs the code that runs as `reticle`. The rule is
# narrow in the direction that matters, which is that it is not root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Restarting one facility's service after the release symlink moves, and again
# on rollback. The wildcard covers the slug and nothing else — sudo matches each
# argument on its own, so `reticle@zmb` matches and `reticle@zmb --now-do-this`
# does not.
reticle-deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart reticle@*

# migrate-all.sh backs up and migrates each facility as the account that owns
# the data. A database URL and a secret key sit in each facility's env file,
# mode 0600 and owned by `reticle`, so this cannot be done as anyone else.
reticle-deploy ALL=(reticle) NOPASSWD: /bin/bash

# Deliberately NOT granted, so that the list is a decision rather than an
# accident:
#   * systemctl daemon-reload, enable, stop  — unit files change with the OS,
#     not with a release, and stopping a facility is a person's decision.
#   * nginx -t / reload  — vhosts are written by provision-facility.sh, which
#     an operator runs as root. A deploy never touches them.
#   * anything as root beyond the one restart above.
